{
  "schema_version": "howl.exploration_result/v1",
  "exploration_id": "hd-20261011-001500-606a90a2e3b1",
  "parent_request_id": "cubs-r004-prompt-v4-review",
  "objective": "Independently review this proposed change to a campaign execution prompt (the candidate is a diff adding six lessons to section 19A). The prompt's own rules for such changes are in evidence id prompt-s18. Check: contradictions with the rest of section 19A (evidence prompt-s19a-v4) or the prompt; whether any line weakens or bypasses public-interface use, independent review, evidence, acceptance or merge gates, the pause-and-help rule, the no-local-LLM rule, isolation, or protections for human work; whether it grants new permissions or spending; recovery gaps; unnecessary burden; vague or untestable wording; and whether each lesson's cited trigger (journal evidence ids) justifies it. Use ASSUMPTION lines for problems, CITE for evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete wording fix.",
  "originating_component": "operator",
  "authority": {
    "type": "ADVISORY",
    "executable": false
  },
  "candidates": [
    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261011-001500-606a90a2e3b1/candidates/0/0",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261011-001500-606a90a2e3b1",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r004-prompt-v4-review",
      "objective": "Independently review this proposed change to a campaign execution prompt (the candidate is a diff adding six lessons to section 19A). The prompt's own rules for such changes are in evidence id prompt-s18. Check: contradictions with the rest of section 19A (evidence prompt-s19a-v4) or the prompt; whether any line weakens or bypasses public-interface use, independent review, evidence, acceptance or merge gates, the pause-and-help rule, the no-local-LLM rule, isolation, or protections for human work; whether it grants new permissions or spending; recovery gaps; unnecessary burden; vague or untestable wording; and whether each lesson's cited trigger (journal evidence ids) justifies it. Use ASSUMPTION lines for problems, CITE for evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete wording fix.",
      "text": "ASSUMPTION: No lesson appears to remove a gate, grant a permission, add spending, or touch the no-local-LLM rule, isolation, or human-work protections, so the problems below concern wording, triggers, and recovery gaps. Whether section 16, section 17 and the \"run contract\" say what the lessons assume is unverified, because those texts were not supplied.\nUNKNOWN: section-16-and-17-text\nUNKNOWN: run-contract-definition\n\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10t16-26-38z\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-20-54-utc\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 1 (fresh-clone gate before PR) has a sound trigger. The clean copy failed (14 failed, 26 errors) while review and acceptance passed in the working tree, and the fresh clone caught manifest-commit drift.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 1 says \"the full gate\", but the existing 19A repair bullet says \"the repository's required regression gate\". Two terms invite a narrower reading.\nCALC: 327 - 321 = 6\nASSUMPTION: R4-S5's working-tree gate had 327 passed and 0 skipped, while its fresh clone had 321 passed and 6 data-dependent skips. Lesson 1 does not say whether skips are acceptable or must be explained, so a clone could \"pass\" with less coverage.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 1 has a recovery gap. If the clone gate fails after Howl acceptance, nothing says the fix must go back through Howl review and acceptance. The 20:54 entry shows the controller restructuring commits itself with `git reset --soft`, and section 18 forbids manual product implementation.\nASSUMPTION: \"git clone of the exact commit\" is not literal, since a clone needs a checkout of that commit. It also does not say whether to clone from the local repository (unpushed commits) or from the remote.\nIDEA: Reword lesson 1 as \"run the repository's required regression gate, as in the --verify bullet, in a fresh clone checked out at the exact commit. Record the commit, the result, and the skip count with the reason for each skip. On failure, record a finding and fix it through Howl with review and acceptance; do not edit the product by hand.\"\n\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-19-49-utc\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-20-39-utc\nCONFLICT: cubs-p-011-mitigation. The 19:49 entry proposes `sh scripts/gate.sh` as the mitigation for quoting refusals. The 20:39 entry says Cursor's rejection of that same command was unrelated to quoting and that Claude's refusals were the regeneration commands, so the evidence for the lesson is mixed.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 2 rests on a finding labelled provisional (CUBS-P-011) from one session (R4-S3). Its later evidence partly undercuts the cause, so the lesson overstates how well a script fixes refusals.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 2 lets the worker create the verification script in its own first task. That risks a weakened gate, which contradicts the 19A rule against narrowing --verify, and the worker would be writing the check on its own work.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 2 has no requirement that the script run exactly the required regression gate, that the script content or hash be recorded, or that reviewers treat later edits to it as a finding. It also does not say how the --verify-timeout bullet applies to a wrapped gate.\nIDEA: Add to lesson 2 \"The script must run the repository's required gate unchanged. Record its commit and hash, and the independent review must confirm it is not narrower. Any in-session edit to it is a finding.\"\nASSUMPTION: Creating the script inside the session's goal spends part of a worker budget (the 1800 s cap appeared in R4-S3). Whether that counts against the session is not stated.\n\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10t15-47-20z\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-22-30-utc\nCONFLICT: lesson-3-trigger. The 15:47:20Z entry records a clean MERGED_VERIFIED with no stacking problem, so it does not support the lesson. Only the 22:30 entry shows stacked PRs landing in parent branches, and it refers to #8/#9 without supplied evidence.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 3 bundles three rules (one PR per run, stacking limits, post-merge verification) under a trigger that supports only the last two.\nASSUMPTION: \"Cannot be reviewed without an earlier unmerged one\" is vague and hard to test. A single PR per run may also make independent review and user review coarser, and R004 used five sessions that produced stacked PRs.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 3 does not say what to do when the user reports a merge that landed in a parent branch. The 22:30 entry shows the recovery (a new PR to main, tree-identical to the verified commit, then a fresh-clone gate), but the prompt omits it.\nIDEA: Add to lesson 3 \"If a change landed in a non-default branch, open a PR to the default branch with an identical tree and verify it from a fresh clone before MERGED_VERIFIED. The user performs all merges.\"\n\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 4 (read CI before handoff) is justified by the 22:30 entry, where local gates and the Howl session's verify and review missed a machine-dependent test failure.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 4 allows \"record it in the PR with a reason\" and then handoff, so the controller could hand over a red PR on its own judgment. This weakens the pause-and-help and merge-gate intent unless the user is explicitly told and the check is never counted as passed.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 4 does not say whether a Howl fix session counts against the session budget. The 22:30 entry labels such a fix \"part of the repair, not a product session\", which could be read as bypassing the budget.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 4 is silent on pending checks, a repository with no CI, flaky checks, or external CI outages.\nIDEA: Reword lesson 4 as \"Read the PR's CI checks before handoff. A failing or pending check is a finding. Fix it through Howl within the existing session budget, or list it as open in the handoff message to the user and do not call the PR passing. If CI is absent or unavailable, record that.\"\n\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-21-40-utc\nASSUMPTION: The 21:40 entry shows screenshot inspection finding readability defects that tests missed, which supports the lesson in substance. It also shows the controller already doing this and recording the defects without calling them passed, so it is a codification more than a response to a failure.\nCONFLICT: lesson-5-sequence. Lesson 5 says \"before accepting\", but in R4-S5 Howl acceptance occurred first and the controller screenshots came afterward. The Howl acceptor, not the controller, accepts.\nASSUMPTION: \"Accepting\" in lesson 5 is ambiguous and could be read as the controller accepting work, which would change authority. It should refer to recording the run outcome or the final assessment, with Howl acceptance unchanged.\nASSUMPTION: \"Each tested viewport\" and \"readability problems\" are loosely defined and hard to test, and the lesson sets no minimum evidence such as stored screenshot paths. If image review used a local model, it would hit the no-local-LLM rule, which the lesson does not address.\n\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10t16-27-03z\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 6 has a justified trigger. The R001 label correction changed two research lines under a \"contract\" that called them unchanged, and the 21:40 entry disclosed the deviation afterward.\nASSUMPTION: \"Record a contract amendment\" does not say who authorizes it. Under section 18 only a user instruction can change mission constraints, so a controller-recorded amendment could be read as self-authorization of changes to inputs the contract calls unchanged.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 6 requires a user decision only for published research results or wording. A label correction that moves a result from \"failed\" to \"not run\" sits near the line against relabeling outcomes, and the evidence shows no user decision before the R001 correction.\nASSUMPTION: Lesson 6 lacks a rule to keep the original wording on record. The v3 label bullet requires that \"the old definition's result is still reported\", and lesson 6 does not repeat it.\nASSUMPTION: \"Before the session\" leaves a recovery gap for a defect found mid-session, as the explorer's screenshot did.\nIDEA: Reword lesson 6 as \"Before the session that would change an input the run contract calls unchanged, pause under section 17 and obtain a dated user decision, then record the amendment and quote the decision. Keep the original text and wording on record and report the change as a deviation.\"\n\nASSUMPTION: The trigger citations mix ISO `T…Z` timestamps and \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM UTC\" forms, while the v3 journal bullet requires times from `date -u`. Citation format should be consistent so each trigger can be found by exact heading.\nASSUMPTION: The header and the \"v4 takes effect at R005\" statement are consistent with section 18, since they activate at a run boundary and add no authority. The v4 diff's hash chain lists the v3 sha256 but gives none for v4, so the new version is not yet recorded as section 18 step 6 requires.\nUNKNOWN: v4-sha256",
