    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261011-001500-606a90a2e3b1/candidates/0/1",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261011-001500-606a90a2e3b1",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r004-prompt-v4-review",
      "objective": "Independently review this proposed change to a campaign execution prompt (the candidate is a diff adding six lessons to section 19A). The prompt's own rules for such changes are in evidence id prompt-s18. Check: contradictions with the rest of section 19A (evidence prompt-s19a-v4) or the prompt; whether any line weakens or bypasses public-interface use, independent review, evidence, acceptance or merge gates, the pause-and-help rule, the no-local-LLM rule, isolation, or protections for human work; whether it grants new permissions or spending; recovery gaps; unnecessary burden; vague or untestable wording; and whether each lesson's cited trigger (journal evidence ids) justifies it. Use ASSUMPTION lines for problems, CITE for evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete wording fix.",
      "text": "ASSUMPTION: Overall, no line expressly removes a gate, grants a permission, or adds spending, and the intro of 19A (\"add no authority, remove no gate\") still holds in form. Each of L1 to L6 below still has a wording or recovery gap. The v4 diff should not be committed until those are addressed and it has passed the supported Howl review path (prompt-s18 step 4). This harness output is not that review.\nCITE: prompt-s18\nCITE: prompt-s19a-v4\nASSUMPTION: L1 (fresh-clone gate before PR) is justified as to trigger. The 16:26:38Z entry shows a clean copy failing (14 failed, 26 errors) while Howl verify, review and acceptance all passed in the working tree, and the 20:54 entry shows a fresh clone exposing a provenance drift failure.\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10t16-26-38z\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-20-54-utc\nCALC: 321 + 6 = 327\nASSUMPTION: L1 calls the clone run \"the full gate\", but the clone ran 321 passed with 6 data-dependent skips, while the working-tree gate ran 327 passed with 0 skipped. The clone is therefore a different and partly narrower gate, which sits uneasily with the v2 rule against narrowing --verify. The lesson should require recording pass and skip counts and treating an unexpected skip count as a finding.\nCONFLICT: l1_full_gate_vs_clone_skips\nASSUMPTION: L1 has a recovery gap. It says nothing about what to do when the clone gate fails after Howl has already accepted. In the 20:54 entry the controller worked around it by splitting its own commit with git reset --soft. Without text requiring a finding and a fix through Howl review and acceptance, a controller could hand-patch product code to get a green clone, which section 18 forbids as manual product implementation.\nASSUMPTION: L1 does not say where the clone comes from (a local path for an unpushed commit, or the remote). It also does not say that a clone made on this machine still shares the machine's installed CLIs, which is the gap the 22:30 CI failure exposed.\nASSUMPTION: The 16:26:38Z journal candidate also asked for a git check-ignore check on new output directories, and the diff drops it. This is probably covered by the repository gate (the 19:49 and 20:39 entries show a \"no ignored paths\" check), but that should be stated.\nCONFLICT: l1_journal_candidate_vs_diff_check_ignore\nASSUMPTION: L2 (short committed gate script) has a trigger that is only provisional and partly contradicted. CUBS-P-011 is labelled provisional. The 19:49 entry attributes the refusals to exact-literal quoting, but the 20:39 entry says Cursor's shell rejection of \"sh scripts/gate.sh\" is unrelated to quoting and recurred in R4-S1 and R4-S3. Claude was not refused the script in only one observed session.\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-19-49-utc\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-20-39-utc\nCONFLICT: cursor_shell_rejection_cause_quoting_vs_unrelated\nASSUMPTION: L2 can weaken the verification gate. The script lives in the repository the worker edits, and \"the first task of the session's goal creates it\" makes the worker write its own check. A worker could then narrow the gate or have the script defined by the work it verifies. This conflicts with the v2 rule that the repair is reviewed against the repository's required regression gate and that --verify is never narrowed.\nASSUMPTION: L2 also forces a new file into every target repository, including ones with an existing short gate command such as make test-full, which is unnecessary burden and a human-work-protection question for non-mission-owned repositories.\nIDEA: Reword L2 to: \"Use a short existing or committed gate command (for example `sh scripts/gate.sh` or `make test-full`) that runs the repository's required gate unchanged. Record its content hash. A script created in the same session is reviewed against the required gate by Howl review, and any session diff to it is a finding.\"\nASSUMPTION: L3 (one PR per repo, default branch, verify merges) is well supported for its check half. The 22:30 entry shows #12, #13 and #14 merged into stacked parent branches rather than main, and says \"the #8 and #9 problem again\".\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-22-30-utc\nASSUMPTION: The 15:47:20Z trigger cited in L3 shows PR #10 merged normally with MERGED_VERIFIED based on file presence on origin/main. It does not show stacking, so it only supports the \"run the gate on a fresh clone before MERGED_VERIFIED\" tightening and not the one-PR rule.\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10t15-47-20z\nASSUMPTION: \"One PR per repository per run\" may conflict with per-session isolation and independent review, because each R004 session produced its own reviewed branch. One large PR raises the human review burden. \"Cannot be reviewed without an earlier unmerged one\" is subjective and hard to test. How section 16 treats PR granularity is not in the supplied evidence.\nASSUMPTION: L3 does not say what to do when a change is found on a non-default branch. It names no status other than MERGED_VERIFIED, and no retarget or follow-up PR step, so there is a recovery gap. In 22:30 the controller landed PR #15 on main, but the lesson does not authorize or describe that.\nIDEA: Reword the L3 PR rule to: \"Target every PR at the default branch. A stacked PR needs a stated reason in its body, and before handoff it must be retargeted or replaced when its parent merges. Record MERGED_VERIFIED only after the change is on the default branch and the gate passes on a fresh clone of it, with skip counts recorded.\"\nASSUMPTION: L4 (read CI checks before handoff) is justified by the 22:30 failure: CI failed on 575067f with \"The selected orchestrator codex is not available\", while local make test-full, Howl verify and Howl review could not catch it.\nASSUMPTION: L4 says nothing about CI that is pending, absent, or unreadable. It does not say the checks must be for the PR head commit. The \"record it in the PR with a reason\" option lets the controller waive a failing check on its own say-so, and the PR body is not a handoff the user is guaranteed to read. The waiver should be limited to failures shown unrelated to the change, with the check named in the handoff message.\nASSUMPTION: L4 could consume Howl sessions. R4-S5 was \"6 of 6\", and the 22:30 fix was recorded as repair, not a product session. The lesson does not say whether a CI-fix session counts against the session budget, and the 19A intro says budgets do not change, so the lesson should say to pause when no budget remains and never raise it.\nCALC: 6 - 6 = 0\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10-21-40-utc\nASSUMPTION: L4 and L3 assume the controller can read GitHub PR checks and clone from GitHub. The 22:30 entry shows a fresh GitHub clone and CI inspection already happened, so no new permission appears to be needed.\nASSUMPTION: L5 (screenshots at each viewport) is justified by the 21:40 entry, where readability defects that tests missed were found: interval fragmentation, -0.00 Brier differences, and squeezed headers at 375 px.\nASSUMPTION: L5 says \"before accepting\", but in 21:40 the screenshots were taken after Howl acceptance, from the clean clone, during final assessment. \"Accepting\" is ambiguous between the Howl acceptance and the controller's run-level acceptance. As written, a finding does not block anything because it may be \"listed as open\", so the lesson is a recording duty and not a gate.\nASSUMPTION: \"Readability problems\" is subjective, and \"inspects\" is untestable. The lesson should require recording the viewport list, the screenshot paths and the findings. It should say the inspection is done by the controller itself and not by a local model, in line with the no-local-LLM rule.\nASSUMPTION: Committed screenshots are images, so the L2 record-count check for third-party records cannot count source records in them. Whether screenshots can carry source examples beyond the journaled allowance is a gap.\nASSUMPTION: L6 (contract amendment before changing a contract-unchanged input) is justified by the 16:27:03Z and 20:39 entries. A published R001 label (\"failed its pre-registered test\") misstated an early-stop non-run, and the correction changed two research lines. The 21:40 entry shows the deviation was only disclosed afterward.\nCITE: journal-2026-10-10t16-27-03z\nASSUMPTION: \"The run contract\" is not defined anywhere in the supplied evidence, so \"an input the run contract calls unchanged\" is untestable as written. The lesson also does not say who authorizes the amendment. A controller-authored amendment could change protected research inputs on self-authored text, which section 18 forbids for anything touching research criteria, results or run history.\nASSUMPTION: L6 has a detection gap. It is a pre-session rule, but the R001 label problem was found after the fact from a screenshot, and the 20:39 controller check of \"research/ diff is exactly two lines\" worked as a post-session diff check. There should be a post-session diff of protected paths, pausing under section 17 if anything is outside the amendment.\nASSUMPTION: The second sentence of L6 (published results or wording need a user decision) is stricter than what the journal shows was done in R004. Whether the user decided before the correction is not recorded in the supplied entries. It should also say the old wording and its result stay on record, so that a correction does not \"erase findings\" or \"relabel failures\" under section 18.\nUNKNOWN: r4_label_correction_user_decision\nUNKNOWN: run_contract_definition_and_location\nUNKNOWN: section16_pr_granularity_policy\nUNKNOWN: v3_sha256_correctness\nUNKNOWN: ci_pending_or_absent_handling\nASSUMPTION: Trigger citations in the v4 lines mix the ISO style (2026-10-10T16:26:38Z) with the \"2026-10-10 20:54 UTC\" style, which matches the journal headings. The v3 rule that heading times come from date -u makes it worth confirming that the minute-resolution headings were generated that way. This is not a defect of the candidate itself.\nASSUMPTION: None of the six lines weakens the pause-and-help rule, the no-local-LLM rule, isolation, or public-interface use as written. L1 and L2 could each weaken independent verification through the gaps above, and L1 could invite controller hand-fixes.",
